/==========================================================
/ Historical Database (HDB), DB maintenance, and IPC
/
/ This file covers:
/
/ - opening a remote KDB connection (IPC)
/ - synchronous and asynchronous communication
/ - querying data from a live HDB
/ - building dynamic queries
/ - functional form of select/update
/ - callback handlers like .z.pg, .z.ps, .z.po, .z.pc
/ - server-side authentication and session tracking
/==========================================================

/------------------------------------------
/ IPC - Inter Process Communication
/------------------------------------------

/ `hopen` opens a connection to a remote KDB server
/ Syntax: h:hopen `:host:port:user:pass
/ Here we connect to a server running on localhost:5000
h:hopen`:localhost:5000:username:password

/ Synchronous query:
/ This sends a query and waits for the result
h"select from trades" / sync

/ Asynchronous query:
/ `neg h` gives a handle to send async messages to remote process
/ Here we send an update to the server without waiting for full response
(neg h)"trades:delete exch from trades"

/------------------------------------------
/ Server setup / DB maintenance basics
/------------------------------------------

/ Start KDB server on port 15000
\p 15000

/ Show current working directory
\pwd

/ Change directory to HDB folder
\cd hdb

/ List files in HDB folder
\ls

/ Load all files in current directory
\l .

/ Show available partitions in the HDB
.Q.PV

/ Simple evaluation example
value "3+5"

d:2026.09.29

/ Build a query dynamically as a string
/ Example: select rows for a given date
value "select from trades where date=",string d

/ A function that builds a dynamic select statement
func:{[s;d;st;et]
  "select from trades where date=",(string d),",sym=",(string s),",time.time within ",(string st)," ",string et
  }

/ Inspect table schema
meta trades

/ `within` checks if a value is within a range
within (st;et)

/ A better function that actually returns a table
func:{[s;d;st;et]
  select from trades where date=d,sym=s,time.time within(st;et)
  }

/ A simplified version for a single symbol and time window
func:{[s;d]
  select from trades where date=d,sym=s,time.time within 09:00 09:01
  }

/ Query by symbol only
func:{[s;d]
  select from trades where date=d,sym=s
  }

/ Example call
func[`IBM;2026.09.29;09:00;09:01]
func[`IBM;2026.09.29]

/ Remove the function from the namespace
delete func from `.

/ Check whether the function still exists
func

/ Direct query
select from trades where date=2026.09.29, sym=`IBM, time.time within 09:00 09:01

/ Same query as a string value
value "select from trades where date=2026.09.29,sym=IBM,time.time within 09:00 09:01"

/------------------------------------------
/ Functional form of select / update
/------------------------------------------

/ Q supports a functional form:
/
/ ?[t;c;b;a]
/
/ t = table
/ c = constraints/filters
/ b = grouping
/ a = aggregation or projection
/

/ Example: select from trades where sym=`IBM
?[trades;enlist(=;`sym;enlist`IBM);0b;()]

/ Example: select from trades where date=2026.09.30 and sym in IBM / AAPL
?[trades;((=;`date;2026.09.30);(in;`sym;enlist`IBM`AAPL));0b;()]

/ Example: project selected columns and rename them
?[trades;((=;`date;2026.09.30);(in;`sym;enlist`IBM`AAPL));0b;
  `stock`venue`buysell`px`sz!`sym`exch`side`price`size]

/ Example: aggregate cost by sym
select cost:sum size*price by sym from trades

/ Functional form of the above aggregation
?[trades;();enlist[`sym]!enlist`sym;enlist[`cost]!enlist(sum;(*;`size;`price))]

/ Inspect parse tree generated by Q
parse"select cost:sum size*price by sym from trades"

/ Same expression in functional form
?[trades;();enlist[`sym]!enlist`sym;enlist[`cost]!enlist(sum;(*;`size;`price))]

/------------------------------------------
/ Functional delete/update
/------------------------------------------

/ Example of functional delete
/ Delete rows where sym=`IBM
memtrades:select from trades
parse"delete from trades where sym=`IBM"

![memtrades;((=;`date;2026.09.30);(=;`sym;enlist`IBM));0b;`symbol$()]

/ Delete columns from a copy of the table
![memtrades;();0b;`exch`side]

/------------------------------------------
/ IPC callback functions
/------------------------------------------

/ `.z.po` is called when a client connects successfully
/ `.z.u` gives the username, `.z.P` gives connection time
.z.po:{show "User ",(string .z.u)," has connected successfully at time: ",string .z.P}

/ Example user authentication map
/ key is username, value is password
user:`ram`shyam`mohan`geeta`sita!("ram";"shyam";"mohan";"geeta";"sita")

/ `.z.pw` is called to validate username/password
/ Returns 1b if authenticated else 0b
.z.pw:{[u;p]
  $[not u in key user;
      [show "user ",(string u)," not authenticated!";:0b];
    p like user[u];
      [show "user ",(string u)," is authenticated!";:1b];
      [show "user ",(string u)," not authenticated due to wrong username/password!";:0b]]
  }

/------------------------------------------
/ Synchronous and asynchronous queries
/------------------------------------------

/ `.z.pg` runs on synchronized query execution
.z.pg:{show "The user: ",(string .z.u)," has sent the synch query: ",x;value x}

/ `.z.ps` runs on asynchronous query execution
.z.ps:{show "The user: ",(string .z.u)," has sent the asynch query: ",x;value x}

/ `.z.w` is the remote handle of the client
/ `.z.pc` is called when a client closes the connection
.z.pc:{show "user ",(string .z.u)," has closed the connection on the handle: ",string x}

/------------------------------------------
/ Session tracking example
/------------------------------------------

/ Create a table to store sessions
sessions:([sessionid:`guid$()] usernme:`symbol$();logintime:`timestamp$();query:();)









/==========================================================
/ Remote call examples and debugging notes
/==========================================================

/ In a client session:
q)h:hopen`::15000
q)h
/ returns a handle id

q)h"tables[]"
/ returns all tables on the remote server

q)h"select from trades where sym in `IBM`MSFT, time within 09:00 09:01"
/
/ This is a remote query over IPC
/
/ `time within 09:00 09:01` means rows whose time is within that range

/ Example of passing a function and arguments remotely
func:{[s;d;st;et]
  select from trades where date=d,sym=s,time within st et
  }

q)h(func;(`IBM;2026.09.29;09:00:00 09:01:01))
/ This is a remote function call
/ But note: the exact argument shape matters in Q

q)h(`func;`IBM;2026.09.29;09:00:00;09:01:01)
/
/ This is a valid remote call when the server has the function `func`

/ It returns rows from the trade table within the time range

/------------------------------------------
/ Important Q reminders
/------------------------------------------

/ - `time within st et` is valid when `st` and `et` are timestamps/duration values
/ - `hopen` returns a handle: a connection to a remote KDB server
/ - `(neg h)` is used for async communication
/ - `.z.pg` and `.z.ps` are server-side callback functions
/ - `.z.po` and `.z.pc` handle connect and disconnect events
/ - `value` evaluates a string as Q code
/ - `parse` shows how Q interprets a query
/ - functional form can be very useful for dynamic querying and programmatic logic

/------------------------------------------
/ Example of server-side dynamic function calls
/------------------------------------------

/ This syntax:
/
/ h(`func;`IBM;2026.09.29;09:00:00;09:01:01)
/
/ means:
/
/ - call remote function named `func
/ - pass arguments: symbol, date, start time, end time
/
/ server executes:
/
/ select from trades where date=d,sym=s,time.time within st et
/
/
/ This is a good pattern when building general-purpose
/ KDB analytics or query services.
/
